  <h1>Types of Nursing Home Neglect</h1>
  <p><em>Reviewed by Aspen Lockwood (AL), Editor-in-Chief. Updated May 2026.</em></p>
  <h2>Pressure sores</h2><p>Stage 3/4 pressure sores are presumptively caused by neglect — they develop when residents are not repositioned at least every 2 hours, kept clean and dry, and provided adequate nutrition. A previously wound-free resident developing stage 3/4 sores is a red flag for systemic care failures.</p>
  <h2>Falls</h2><p>Every resident should have a fall risk assessment on admission and updated regularly. High-risk residents need bed alarms, non-slip footwear, call lights within reach, and supervision. Falls are preventable in most cases. Hip fractures in the elderly have a one-year mortality rate exceeding 25%.</p>
  <h2>Malnutrition and dehydration</h2><p>Warning signs: rapid weight loss, skin tenting (dehydration), lethargy, wounds that won&rsquo;t heal. Weight should be monitored monthly. Facilities must document food and fluid intake for at-risk residents. Malnutrition requiring hospitalization in a nursing home resident is almost always a care failure.</p>
  <h2>Medication errors</h2><p>Facilities must maintain accurate medication administration records (MARs). Compare the MAR to the prescription and ask for a medication reconciliation if errors are suspected. Medication errors are frequently underreported. A pharmacist consultant&rsquo;s review of the MAR can identify patterns.</p>
  <h2>Abuse</h2><p>Physical, emotional, and sexual abuse are more common than reported because victims fear retaliation or lack cognitive capacity to report. Warning signs: unexplained bruising, fear of specific staff, withdrawal, reluctance to speak in front of staff. Trust your instincts and report to the ombudsman immediately.</p>
  <h2>Where to go from here</h2>
  <p>See <a href="/guides/what-to-do-after-nursing-home-neglect">what to do after neglect</a>.</p>
